// EY question bank — 100 questions.
// Item shape: { id, cat, q, opts[], ans (0-based index into opts), exp, passage?, img?, imgLabel?, note?, src }.
// Keep ids stable: saved progress and tutor chats are keyed by them. Update data/registry.js if you add or remove items.
window.PREP_DATA = window.PREP_DATA || {};
window.PREP_DATA["ey"] = [{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Which of the following pairs are brothers?","opts":["Amanda and Mandy","Amanda and Augustine","Sandy and Freddy","Curry and Mandy"],"ans":2,"exp":"Sandy is not Curry’s mom, so Sandy is Curry’s father — male. Freddy is Sandy’s brother, so Sandy and Freddy are both male siblings: brothers. Amanda is Sandy’s wife (female), so pairs with Amanda are not brothers; Mandy is a daughter.","passage":"A family consists of six members of the same lineage: Amanda, Curry, Sandy, Mandy, Freddy and Augustine. Curry is a child of Sandy, however Sandy is not the mom of Curry. Amanda and Sandy are married to each other, while Freddy is the brother of Sandy. Mandy is the daughter of Amanda, while Augustine is brother to Amanda.","src":"EY_BANK#0","id":"ey-0"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"What is the relationship between Curry and Mandy?","opts":["Siblings","Uncle and niece","Father and child","Husband and wife"],"ans":0,"exp":"Sandy (the father) and Amanda are married. Curry is Sandy’s child and Mandy is Amanda’s daughter, so Curry and Mandy are children of the same couple: siblings.","passage":"A family consists of six members of the same lineage: Amanda, Curry, Sandy, Mandy, Freddy and Augustine. Curry is a child of Sandy, however Sandy is not the mom of Curry. Amanda and Sandy are married to each other, while Freddy is the brother of Sandy. Mandy is the daughter of Amanda, while Augustine is brother to Amanda.","src":"EY_BANK#1","id":"ey-1"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Which school is in the middle (4th position)?","opts":["Onerous","Villeday","Jonville","Sharon"],"ans":0,"exp":"Chain the clues: Lilleday < Sharon < Jonville (Sharon is right of Lilleday, left of Jonville); Jonville < Onerous < Villeday; Villeday < Touchstone < Aiwa. Left to right: Lilleday, Sharon, Jonville, Onerous, Villeday, Touchstone, Aiwa. The 4th of 7 is Onerous.","passage":"One children’s day, seven schools are scheduled for a march past for the Governor of Logomba State. They are all arranged across the field in a row. Onerous school is found to the left of Villeday school and to the right of Jonville school. Sharon school is fixed to the right of Lilleday and left of Jonville school. Following the pattern, Touchstone is situated to the right of Villeday school and to the left of Aiwa school.","src":"EY_BANK#3","id":"ey-2"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"In a queue, Tom is 7th from the front and 11th from the back. How many people are in the queue?","opts":["16","17","18","19"],"ans":1,"exp":"Total = position from front + position from back − 1 = 7 + 11 − 1 = 17 people.","src":"EY_BANK#5","id":"ey-3"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"If it rains → the match is cancelled. If the match is cancelled → the team goes home early. It rained on Saturday.\n\nWhat can we conclude?","opts":["The team played on Saturday","The team went home early on Saturday","The match was not cancelled","Nothing can be concluded"],"ans":1,"exp":"Chain: rain → match cancelled → team goes home early. Since it rained, by applying both rules in sequence, the team went home early on Saturday.","src":"EY_BANK#6","id":"ey-4"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"If they stand from shortest to tallest, who is in the middle?","opts":["Clinton","Trump","Craig","Mohammed","Bob"],"ans":2,"exp":"Mohammed is shortest and Bob tallest. Trump is only “a bit taller” than Mohammed and Craig only “a bit shorter” than Clinton, so the order is Mohammed, Trump, Craig, Clinton, Bob. The middle (3rd) is Craig.","passage":"There are five friends (Trump, Clinton, Bob, Craig and Mohammed) who kept arguing about who was the tallest or the shortest due to the closeness of their heights.\n• Trump is shorter than Clinton but a bit taller than Mohammed\n• Bob is the tallest\n• Craig is a bit shorter than Clinton but taller than Mohammed","src":"EY_BANK#7","id":"ey-5"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"On what day does Augustine audition?","opts":["Thursday","Friday","Tuesday","Saturday"],"ans":1,"exp":"Amanda = Monday (first day). Mandy = Wednesday (middle). Sandy = Tuesday (just before Mandy). Freddy is after Mandy and Augustine sits between Freddy and Valentine, who is on the weekend: Freddy Thursday, Augustine Friday, Valentine Saturday, leaving Curry on Sunday.","passage":"A film company is holding auditions on all seven days of one week (Monday to Sunday). Valentine, Augustine, Freddy, Mandy, Sandy, Amanda and Curry are lined up, one per day.\n• Mandy is to audition during the middle of the week\n• Valentine is to audition on a weekend\n• Sandy is to audition just before Mandy\n• Amanda is to audition on the first day of the week\n• Freddy is to audition after Mandy\n• Augustine should come between Freddy and Valentine","src":"EY_BANK#8","id":"ey-6"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"How many people are faster than Solomon?","opts":["2","No one","4","1"],"ans":1,"exp":"Speed order (fastest first): Solomon > Dada > Akinbode > Akinfala > Kehinde. The survival facts are distractors. Solomon is fastest, so no one is faster than him.","passage":"Five brothers wish to undertake a trip to the Kalahari desert. The following is a measure of how well each is able to run in the desert for 7 days straight.\n• Akinbode is faster than Akinfala\n• Dada is faster than Akinbode but slower than Solomon\n• Solomon is faster than Kehinde, who is slower than Akinfala\n• Akinfala can last three days without water\n• Akinbode can last 4 days without food and water\n• Someone dies before the end of the 7th day","src":"EY_BANK#9","id":"ey-7"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Look at the series: 8, 11, 9, 12, … What number should come next?","opts":["11","15","14","10"],"ans":3,"exp":"Two interleaved sequences: 8, 9, 10… and 11, 12, 13… alternated: 8, 11, 9, 12, 10, 13… The next term (first sub-sequence after 9) is 10.","src":"EY_BANK#10","id":"ey-8"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Look at this series: 90, 87, 84, 81, 78, 75, … What number comes next?","opts":["72","69","75","80"],"ans":0,"exp":"Each term decreases by 3: 90−3=87, 87−3=84, … 75−3=72. The next number is 72.","src":"EY_BANK#11","id":"ey-9"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Statements:\n• Some princes are black\n• All blacks are cruel\n• Some contractors are black\n\nConclusion I: All contractors are princes\nConclusion II: Some blacks are cruel\n\nWhich conclusion is correct?","opts":["Only conclusion I is true","Either I or II is true","Both conclusions are true","Neither conclusion is true","Only conclusion II is true"],"ans":4,"exp":"Conclusion I is FALSE — some contractors are black doesn't mean any contractor is a prince. Conclusion II is TRUE — ALL blacks are cruel, so SOME blacks are cruel. Only II is true.","src":"EY_BANK#12","id":"ey-10"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"All audit managers at EY have at least 5 years of experience. James has 7 years of experience. Which conclusion is valid?","opts":["James is definitely an audit manager","James may or may not be an audit manager","James cannot be an audit manager","7 years guarantees a managerial role"],"ans":1,"exp":"Having 5+ years is necessary but not sufficient for being an audit manager. James meets the threshold but could be in any role.","src":"EY_BANK#13","id":"ey-11"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"All Blips are Blops. Some Blops are Blings.\n\nWhich conclusion MUST be true?","opts":["All Blips are Blings","Some Blips may be Blings","No Blips are Blings","All Blings are Blips"],"ans":1,"exp":"Since all Blips are Blops, and only SOME Blops are Blings, it is possible but not certain that some Blips are also Blings. 'May be' is the only logically sound conclusion.","src":"EY_BANK#14","id":"ey-12"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Nine partners at a hedge fund. Salary relationships:\n• Caroline > Adrian and Jenner\n• Jenner > Cynthia\n• Adrian > Moshood > Cinnamon > Adamu > Dennis > Sigmund\n\nWhat is the MINIMUM number of unique salaries?","opts":["7","8","6","9"],"ans":0,"exp":"Adrian > Moshood > Cinnamon > Adamu > Dennis > Sigmund is a strict chain of 6 salaries, and Caroline is above Adrian — 7 distinct salaries. Jenner only has to be below Caroline and above Cynthia, so Jenner and Cynthia can share salaries already in the chain (e.g. Jenner = Adrian, Cynthia = Moshood). Minimum = 7.","src":"EY_BANK#15","id":"ey-13"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"A company wants to increase its sales force by 20% and lower non-sales staff by 7.5%. Currently: 30 sales personnel and 120 non-sales personnel. What is the total staff count after the changes?","opts":["147","149","151","153"],"ans":0,"exp":"New sales staff: 30 × 1.20 = 36. New non-sales staff: 120 × 0.925 = 111. Total = 36 + 111 = 147.","src":"EY_BANK#16","id":"ey-14"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Exchange rates: 1 Naira (₦) = 143.37 Japanese yen (¥). 1 euro (€) = 125.38 ¥. How much in Naira is €1,827?","opts":["₦1,348.50","₦1,483.00","₦1,597.75","₦1,718.25"],"ans":2,"exp":"Step 1: €1,827 × 125.38 = ¥229,069.26. Step 2: ¥229,069.26 ÷ 143.37 = ₦1,597.75.","src":"EY_BANK#17","id":"ey-15"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Staff turnover at HCG Ltd in 2012 was 15%. 20% of departing staff were made redundant. By end of 2012 there were 3,827 employees. How many were made redundant during 2012?","opts":["90","105","120","135"],"ans":3,"exp":"Start-of-year staff = 3,827 ÷ 0.85 = 4,502. Staff who left = 15% × 4,502 = 675. Redundancies = 20% × 675 = 135.","src":"EY_BANK#18","id":"ey-16"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Aisha sold 3,570 units in March. Products A, B and C in ratio 10:5:2. How many units of B and C combined were sold?","opts":["1,470 units","1,580 units","1,690 units","1,800 units"],"ans":0,"exp":"Total ratio parts = 17. B+C = 7 parts. (7/17) × 3,570 = 1,470 units.","src":"EY_BANK#19","id":"ey-17"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"After training, an employee generates ₦1,050/week — a 5% increase. Training cost ₦3,000. How many weeks to cover training cost from extra revenue?","opts":["50 weeks","55 weeks","60 weeks","65 weeks"],"ans":2,"exp":"Pre-training weekly revenue = ₦1,050 ÷ 1.05 = ₦1,000. Extra revenue/week = ₦50. Weeks to recover = ₦3,000 ÷ ₦50 = 60 weeks.","src":"EY_BANK#20","id":"ey-18"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"A laptop costs ₦150,000 and is sold at a 15% discount. What is the selling price?","opts":["₦127,500","₦130,000","₦125,000","₦122,500"],"ans":0,"exp":"Discount = 15% × ₦150,000 = ₦22,500. Selling price = ₦150,000 − ₦22,500 = ₦127,500.","src":"EY_BANK#21","id":"ey-19"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"The ratio of male to female employees is 3:2. Total employees = 120. How many are female?","opts":["72","48","60","40"],"ans":1,"exp":"Female share = 2/(3+2) = 2/5. Female count = (2/5) × 120 = 48.","src":"EY_BANK#22","id":"ey-20"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"A can complete a task in 10 days; B in 15 days. How many days working together?","opts":["5 days","6 days","7 days","8 days"],"ans":1,"exp":"Combined rate = 1/10 + 1/15 = 5/30 = 1/6 per day. Together they finish in 6 days.","src":"EY_BANK#23","id":"ey-21"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"₦200,000 invested at 8% per annum simple interest. Total amount after 4 years?","opts":["₦260,000","₦264,000","₦268,000","₦256,000"],"ans":1,"exp":"Interest = 200,000 × 0.08 × 4 = ₦64,000. Total = ₦200,000 + ₦64,000 = ₦264,000.","src":"EY_BANK#24","id":"ey-22"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"A trader buys goods for ₦80,000 and sells them for ₦92,000. What is the profit percentage?","opts":["12%","13%","15%","10%"],"ans":2,"exp":"Profit = ₦12,000. Profit % = (₦12,000 ÷ ₦80,000) × 100 = 15%.","src":"EY_BANK#25","id":"ey-23"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"₦100,000 invested at 10% per annum compound interest. Value after 2 years?","opts":["₦120,000","₦121,000","₦122,000","₦119,000"],"ans":1,"exp":"Year 1: 100,000 × 1.1 = ₦110,000. Year 2: 110,000 × 1.1 = ₦121,000.","src":"EY_BANK#26","id":"ey-24"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Gareth's weekly budget = ₦250. ½ on rent, 12.5% on bills, 27.5% on food. ¾ of leftover goes to savings each week. How many weeks to save ₦900?","opts":["42 weeks","44 weeks","46 weeks","48 weeks"],"ans":3,"exp":"Rent=₦125, Bills=₦31.25, Food=₦68.75. Total accounted=₦225. Leftover=₦25. Weekly savings=¾×25=₦18.75. Weeks=₦900÷₦18.75=48 weeks.","src":"EY_BANK#27","id":"ey-25"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"A bag contains only red and blue marbles. There are 4 times as many red marbles as blue marbles. If the total number of marbles is 40, what is the probability of drawing 2 blue marbles in a row without replacement?","opts":["1/25","7/195","2/39","1/26"],"ans":1,"exp":"Blue = 40 ÷ 5 = 8 (red is 4 × blue). P(blue, then blue) = 8/40 × 7/39 = 56/1,560 = 7/195.","note":"The previous key was 2/39, which does not equal 8/40 × 7/39. Options corrected so the true answer, 7/195, is listed.","src":"EY_BANK#28","id":"ey-26"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"What is the next number in the sequence?\n4, 7, 12, 19, 28, ___","opts":["35","37","39","41"],"ans":2,"exp":"Differences: +3, +5, +7, +9, +11 (odd numbers, increasing by 2 each step). Next term = 28 + 11 = 39.","src":"EY_BANK#29","id":"ey-27"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Find the odd one out:\n16, 25, 36, 48, 64, 81","opts":["25","36","48","64"],"ans":2,"exp":"16=4², 25=5², 36=6², 64=8², 81=9². 48 is NOT a perfect square. 48 is the odd one out.","src":"EY_BANK#30","id":"ey-28"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"What comes next?\n1, 2, 4, 8, 16, ___","opts":["24","28","32","36"],"ans":2,"exp":"Each term is doubled: 16×2=32.","src":"EY_BANK#31","id":"ey-29"},{"cat":"Data Interpretation","q":"A company's annual costs: Salaries 45%, Raw materials 25%, Overheads 15%, Marketing 10%, Miscellaneous 5%. Total annual cost = ₦20 million. How much MORE is spent on Salaries than on Marketing?","opts":["₦6 million","₦7 million","₦8 million","₦9 million"],"ans":1,"exp":"Salaries = 45% × ₦20m = ₦9m. Marketing = 10% × ₦20m = ₦2m. Difference = ₦7 million.","src":"EY_BANK#32","id":"ey-30"},{"cat":"Data Interpretation","q":"A project team of 5 people has the following daily rates: Ada ₦15,000, Bayo ₦12,000, Chidi ₦18,000, Dami ₦10,000, Eze ₦14,000. What is the average daily rate across the team?","opts":["₦12,800","₦13,600","₦13,800","₦14,200"],"ans":2,"exp":"Total = 15,000+12,000+18,000+10,000+14,000 = 69,000. Average = 69,000 ÷ 5 = ₦13,800.","src":"EY_BANK#33","id":"ey-31"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Choose the word most similar in meaning to UBIQUITOUS.","opts":["Rare","Omnipresent","Fragile","Dominant"],"ans":1,"exp":"Ubiquitous means present, appearing, or found everywhere. Omnipresent is its closest synonym.","src":"EY_BANK#34","id":"ey-32"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Choose the word most opposite in meaning to BENEVOLENT.","opts":["Generous","Malevolent","Indifferent","Passive"],"ans":1,"exp":"Benevolent means well-meaning and kindly. Its antonym is malevolent — wishing harm to others.","src":"EY_BANK#35","id":"ey-33"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"'The new policy will have far-reaching implications.' The word 'implications' means:","opts":["Direct orders","Possible consequences","Written rules","Official explanations"],"ans":1,"exp":"Implications are the possible consequences or conclusions that can be drawn from something, even if not directly stated.","src":"EY_BANK#36","id":"ey-34"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Select the correctly punctuated sentence:","opts":["Its a good day for a walk.","It's a good day for a walk.","Its' a good day for a walk.","Its a good day for a walk!"],"ans":1,"exp":"'It's' is the contraction of 'it is'. The apostrophe replaces the missing letter 'i'. 'Its' (without apostrophe) is the possessive form.","src":"EY_BANK#37","id":"ey-35"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"All companies that expanded internationally in the last decade reported higher profits. XYZ Ltd. reported higher profits last year.\n\nConclusion: XYZ Ltd. expanded internationally.\n\nWhich of the following best describes this argument?","opts":["Valid — the conclusion follows necessarily","Invalid — higher profits could have other causes","Valid — expansion always causes higher profits","Invalid — the premise is false"],"ans":1,"exp":"This is a logical fallacy (affirming the consequent). Higher profits could result from many things besides international expansion. The conclusion does not follow necessarily.","src":"EY_BANK#38","id":"ey-36"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"A study found that people who drink coffee daily score higher on focus tests. Therefore, drinking coffee improves focus.\n\nWhich of the following most seriously weakens this argument?","opts":["Some coffee drinkers are not focused","People with naturally high focus may be more likely to drink coffee","Coffee is expensive","Not all focus tests are reliable"],"ans":1,"exp":"If people with naturally high focus tend to drink coffee, then the correlation reflects existing traits rather than coffee causing improved focus. This is reverse causation — it directly undermines the causal claim.","src":"EY_BANK#39","id":"ey-37"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"A publishing company earns revenue from three areas: Digital products = 30% of sales, 40% of profits. Books = 60% of sales, 50% of profits. Audiobooks/other = 10% of sales, 10% of profits.\n\nWhich of the following can be INFERRED from these figures?","opts":["Digital products earn higher profit per sale than books","Digital products earn more total profit than books","The digital products segment is growing faster than books","Each individual digital product is more profitable than each book"],"ans":0,"exp":"Digital products make up 30% of sales but 40% of profits — a higher profit share than sales share. Books make up 60% of sales but only 50% of profits. This directly implies digital products earn higher profit per unit sold than books.","src":"EY_BANK#40","id":"ey-38"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"Springfield's metro upgrade proposal promises a 20% increase in passenger capacity, reducing the need for private car use and solving traffic congestion.\n\nWhich of the following, if true, most SERIOUSLY WEAKENS this argument?","opts":["Most Springfield residents are unwilling to switch from driving to public transit","Increasing metro capacity will require trains to travel more slowly","The proposed metro upgrade will increase air pollution","The metro is currently not filled to capacity during rush hour"],"ans":0,"exp":"Even if the metro expands capacity, if most drivers are unwilling to switch, the expansion won't reduce car usage and therefore won't ease traffic. The plan's premise collapses if people simply refuse to use the metro.","src":"EY_BANK#41","id":"ey-39"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"Vegetarians tend to live longer than meat-eaters. However, this does not show vegetarianism CAUSES longer life, because vegetarians also tend to exercise more regularly.\n\nWhich of the following, if true, most STRENGTHENS this argument?","opts":["A vegetarian who doesn't exercise is more likely to start exercising than a meat-eater","Among vegetarians, those who exercise early in life maintain that habit","Among people who exercise regularly, those who don't eat meat live as long as those who do","Vegetarianism causes people to adopt other healthy habits besides exercise"],"ans":2,"exp":"If people who exercise regularly live equally long regardless of whether they eat meat (option C), this strongly supports the idea that it's the exercise, not the diet, that explains vegetarians' longer lives.","src":"EY_BANK#42","id":"ey-40"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"My grandfather showed me a family portrait and pointed out a young man who was the brother of my father’s wife’s sister. Who is this person to my grandfather?","opts":["Great grandson","Son in law","Nephew","Son"],"ans":3,"exp":"My father’s wife is my mother. The brother of my mother’s sister is my mother’s brother (my maternal uncle). If the grandfather is my mother’s father, her brother is his son.","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-41"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"If every applicant interviewed is hired, and if Lucy is hired, then each of the following applicants must be interviewed EXCEPT:","opts":["Raskos","Sarada","Kane","Clark"],"ans":1,"exp":"Kane is interviewed, so Kane is hired, which requires Raskos to be interviewed (and hired), which requires Clark to be interviewed (and hired). Clark hired + Lucy interviewed means Christian is hired. So Kane, Raskos, Clark, Lucy and Christian must all be interviewed. Nothing forces Sarada.","passage":"Kane, Sarada, Valery, Raskos, Lucy, Clark and Christian are the applicants being considered for positions at XYZ Limited. Only applicants who have been interviewed will be hired. The following constraints must be met:\n• If Valery is interviewed, Raskos is interviewed as well\n• If Raskos is interviewed, Lucy is interviewed\n• Kane is interviewed\n• Kane is not hired unless Raskos is interviewed\n• Raskos is not hired unless Clark is interviewed\n• If Clark is hired and Lucy is interviewed, Christian is hired","note":"In the original screenshot option D was Valery, who is also not forced — making two correct answers. Option D was changed to Clark so the question has one answer.","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-42"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"If Sarada and five other applicants are the only applicants interviewed, and exactly three applicants are hired, which of the following could be the list of applicants hired?","opts":["Clark, Raskos and Kane","Clark, Lucy and Kane","Sarada, Valery and Clark","Sarada, Valery and Christian"],"ans":3,"exp":"A: Raskos interviewed forces Lucy interviewed; Clark hired + Lucy interviewed forces Christian hired — four hires. B: same problem (Clark hired, Lucy interviewed). C: Valery forces Raskos and Lucy to be interviewed; Clark hired then forces Christian — four hires. D works: interview Kane, Sarada, Valery, Raskos, Lucy and Christian (Clark not interviewed); hire Sarada, Valery, Christian. Kane and Raskos are interviewed but not hired, and with Clark not hired the Christian rule never fires.","passage":"Kane, Sarada, Valery, Raskos, Lucy, Clark and Christian are the applicants being considered for positions at XYZ Limited. Only applicants who have been interviewed will be hired. The following constraints must be met:\n• If Valery is interviewed, Raskos is interviewed as well\n• If Raskos is interviewed, Lucy is interviewed\n• Kane is interviewed\n• Kane is not hired unless Raskos is interviewed\n• Raskos is not hired unless Clark is interviewed\n• If Clark is hired and Lucy is interviewed, Christian is hired","note":"The original screenshot says “Shany”, which is not one of the applicants; it is read as Sarada. Option B originally repeated option A, so it was replaced.","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-43"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"If Christian is not interviewed, and exactly four applicants are hired, which of the following must be false?","opts":["Valery is hired","Raskos is interviewed","Lucy is hired","Clark is hired"],"ans":3,"exp":"If Clark were hired, Lucy could not be interviewed (or Christian would have to be hired). Then Raskos and Valery could not be interviewed either (each forces Lucy), and Kane cannot be hired without Raskos. That leaves at most Sarada and Clark hired — not four. So “Clark is hired” must be false. The others are possible, e.g. hire Sarada, Kane, Raskos and Lucy with Clark interviewed but not hired.","passage":"Kane, Sarada, Valery, Raskos, Lucy, Clark and Christian are the applicants being considered for positions at XYZ Limited. Only applicants who have been interviewed will be hired. The following constraints must be met:\n• If Valery is interviewed, Raskos is interviewed as well\n• If Raskos is interviewed, Lucy is interviewed\n• Kane is interviewed\n• Kane is not hired unless Raskos is interviewed\n• Raskos is not hired unless Clark is interviewed\n• If Clark is hired and Lucy is interviewed, Christian is hired","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-44"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Which of these schools CAN be found among the three schools on the right side of the row?","opts":["Touchstone","Lilleday","Jonville","Sharon"],"ans":0,"exp":"Chain the clues: Lilleday < Sharon < Jonville (Sharon is right of Lilleday, left of Jonville); Jonville < Onerous < Villeday; Villeday < Touchstone < Aiwa. Left to right: Lilleday, Sharon, Jonville, Onerous, Villeday, Touchstone, Aiwa. The three on the right are Villeday, Touchstone and Aiwa.","passage":"One children’s day, seven schools are scheduled for a march past for the Governor of Logomba State. They are all arranged across the field in a row. Onerous school is found to the left of Villeday school and to the right of Jonville school. Sharon school is fixed to the right of Lilleday and left of Jonville school. Following the pattern, Touchstone is situated to the right of Villeday school and to the left of Aiwa school.","note":"The original asked which school “cannot” be among the three on the right; Lilleday, Jonville and Sharon all qualify, so it had three correct answers. Reworded so the question has one answer.","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-45"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Which school is at the right-hand end of the row?","opts":["Sharon","Aiwa","Lilleday","Jonville"],"ans":1,"exp":"Chain the clues: Lilleday < Sharon < Jonville (Sharon is right of Lilleday, left of Jonville); Jonville < Onerous < Villeday; Villeday < Touchstone < Aiwa. Left to right: Lilleday, Sharon, Jonville, Onerous, Villeday, Touchstone, Aiwa. Aiwa is at the right-hand end.","passage":"One children’s day, seven schools are scheduled for a march past for the Governor of Logomba State. They are all arranged across the field in a row. Onerous school is found to the left of Villeday school and to the right of Jonville school. Sharon school is fixed to the right of Lilleday and left of Jonville school. Following the pattern, Touchstone is situated to the right of Villeday school and to the left of Aiwa school.","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-46"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Who is the tallest in the group?","opts":["Lashley","None of the options","Charity","Jennifer"],"ans":1,"exp":"Lashley is second tallest, so not tallest. Cherie is taller than Charity and Jennifer, so neither is tallest. The tallest must be Natasha or Cherie — neither is listed, so “None of the options”.","passage":"There is a group of girls (five of them).\n• Lashley is the second tallest but is younger than Cherie\n• Charity is a bit taller than Jennifer but is younger than her\n• Cherie and Jennifer are around the same age, but Cherie is taller than both of them\n• Natasha is taller than Charity and older than Cherie","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-47"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"Which of the following, if correct, provides the strongest support for the explanation?","opts":["Children do not recover as fast from a sickness as adults do.","Children are always more likely to refuse eating unfamiliar foods compared to adults.","More distinctive flavours have a way of making children dislike them, whether they cause sickness or not.","Compared to adults, children are more likely to see a connection between their health and the food they eat."],"ans":3,"exp":"The explanation works only if children link falling ill to something they ate. D says children are especially likely to make that food–health connection, which supports the mechanism. C offers a different cause (distinctive flavours alone), which competes with the explanation; A and B are irrelevant to the sickness–aversion link.","passage":"It is not impossible to say that someone who falls sick as a result of eating a particular meal will eventually develop a strong aversion for the one food in that meal that has the most unique taste, whether or not that food caused the sickness. This surely explains why children are likely to develop strong distaste for certain food types.","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-48"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Which conclusion follows?","opts":["Conclusion I and II are true","Either conclusion I or II is true","Neither conclusion I nor II is true","Only conclusion II is true","Only conclusion I is true"],"ans":4,"exp":"The flashy phones are heavy, so some phones are heavy (I follows). Nothing links lasting things to phones — the only fact about lasting is that flashy things are not lasting — so II does not follow.","passage":"Statements:\n• Some phones are flashy\n• All flashy are heavy\n• No flashy is lasting\nConclusion I: Some phones are heavy\nConclusion II: Some lasting are phones","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-49"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Which kid is not hardworking?","opts":["Jayden","Jason","Joshua","Samuel","None of the options"],"ans":4,"exp":"“Hardworking” is not one of the listed traits (smart, trustworthy, vindictive, caring), so no son can be identified as not hardworking.","passage":"A mother is told to describe her sons in a television show. Her short, terse description goes like this:\n• Samuel, Jason and Joshua are smart\n• Samuel, Jayden and Foster are trustworthy\n• Jayden, Joshua and Foster are vindictive\n• Samuel, Jason and Foster are caring","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-50"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Which kid had the most qualities?","opts":["Samuel","None of the options","Jason","Foster"],"ans":1,"exp":"Samuel: smart, trustworthy, caring (3). Foster: trustworthy, vindictive, caring (3). Jason, Joshua and Jayden have 2 each. Samuel and Foster tie, so no single kid has the most — “None of the options”.","passage":"A mother is told to describe her sons in a television show. Her short, terse description goes like this:\n• Samuel, Jason and Joshua are smart\n• Samuel, Jayden and Foster are trustworthy\n• Jayden, Joshua and Foster are vindictive\n• Samuel, Jason and Foster are caring","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-51"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Which of the following is true for Osteen?","opts":["Incomplete information","None of the options","Kuje-Bus","Ibadan-Boat","Ibadan-Bus"],"ans":0,"exp":"Modes: Richard–car (to Zamfara), Pascal–plane, Osteen–boat, Terrence–train, so Jason–bus. The bus cannot go to Kuje or Ibadan, and Zamfara is Richard’s, so Jason goes to Kano or Zola. Kuje cannot be reached by boat, bus or car, so Kuje is reached by plane (Pascal) or train (Terrence). Osteen (boat) cannot go to Kuje or Zamfara, but could go to Ibadan, Kano or Zola — the clues do not decide which. So the information is incomplete.","passage":"Five friends (Jason, Pascal, Richard, Osteen and Terrence) were vacationing in different cities — Ibadan, Kano, Kuje, Zamfara and Zola — and travelled from Lagos by car, boat, plane, train and bus, one mode each.\n• Whoever is to travel to Kuje cannot do so by boat\n• Richard journeyed to Zamfara by car while the plane was preferred by Pascal\n• Osteen was keen on the boat while Terrence chose to travel by train\n• Kuje and Ibadan cannot be reached from Lagos by bus","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-52"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Which of the following pairs of destination/mode of journey is NOT accurate?","opts":["Kuje-Bus","Zamfara-Car","Ibadan-Boat","Kano-Plane","Zola-Bus"],"ans":0,"exp":"Modes: Richard–car (to Zamfara), Pascal–plane, Osteen–boat, Terrence–train, so Jason–bus. The bus cannot go to Kuje or Ibadan, and Zamfara is Richard’s, so Jason goes to Kano or Zola. Kuje cannot be reached by boat, bus or car, so Kuje is reached by plane (Pascal) or train (Terrence). Kuje cannot be reached by bus, so Kuje-Bus cannot be accurate. Zamfara-Car is given; the other pairs are possible.","passage":"Five friends (Jason, Pascal, Richard, Osteen and Terrence) were vacationing in different cities — Ibadan, Kano, Kuje, Zamfara and Zola — and travelled from Lagos by car, boat, plane, train and bus, one mode each.\n• Whoever is to travel to Kuje cannot do so by boat\n• Richard journeyed to Zamfara by car while the plane was preferred by Pascal\n• Osteen was keen on the boat while Terrence chose to travel by train\n• Kuje and Ibadan cannot be reached from Lagos by bus","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-53"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Geography is being taught by how many teachers?","opts":["None of the options","3","2","1"],"ans":2,"exp":"Subjects per teacher: Tatiana — Physics, English, Geography, History, French (5); Ben — Physics, English, Mathematics (3); Cody — English, Geography (2); Daniel — Mathematics, Physics (2); Saka — History, French (2). Geography appears only in “Cody and Tatiana teach English and Geography”: 2 teachers.","passage":"A new secondary school just opened in your area and started off with 5 teachers teaching 6 subjects (Physics, English, Geography, Mathematics, History, French). The combinations of teachers and subjects are:\n• Ben and Tatiana teach Physics and English\n• Cody and Tatiana teach English and Geography\n• Daniel and Ben teach Mathematics and Physics\n• Saka and Tatiana teach History and French","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-54"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"If you add up the number of subjects taken by Saka and Tatiana, what number would it amount to?","opts":["8","6","5","7"],"ans":3,"exp":"Subjects per teacher: Tatiana — Physics, English, Geography, History, French (5); Ben — Physics, English, Mathematics (3); Cody — English, Geography (2); Daniel — Mathematics, Physics (2); Saka — History, French (2). Tatiana 5 + Saka 2 = 7.","passage":"A new secondary school just opened in your area and started off with 5 teachers teaching 6 subjects (Physics, English, Geography, Mathematics, History, French). The combinations of teachers and subjects are:\n• Ben and Tatiana teach Physics and English\n• Cody and Tatiana teach English and Geography\n• Daniel and Ben teach Mathematics and Physics\n• Saka and Tatiana teach History and French","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-55"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Who takes the second least number of subjects?","opts":["Cody","None of the options","Saka","Ben"],"ans":3,"exp":"Subjects per teacher: Tatiana — Physics, English, Geography, History, French (5); Ben — Physics, English, Mathematics (3); Cody — English, Geography (2); Daniel — Mathematics, Physics (2); Saka — History, French (2). The least is 2 (Cody, Daniel, Saka); the next level is 3 — Ben.","passage":"A new secondary school just opened in your area and started off with 5 teachers teaching 6 subjects (Physics, English, Geography, Mathematics, History, French). The combinations of teachers and subjects are:\n• Ben and Tatiana teach Physics and English\n• Cody and Tatiana teach English and Geography\n• Daniel and Ben teach Mathematics and Physics\n• Saka and Tatiana teach History and French","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-56"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Which teacher does not take English, Physics or Mathematics, but takes French?","opts":["Saka","Ben","Cody","Tatiana"],"ans":0,"exp":"Subjects per teacher: Tatiana — Physics, English, Geography, History, French (5); Ben — Physics, English, Mathematics (3); Cody — English, Geography (2); Daniel — Mathematics, Physics (2); Saka — History, French (2). Only Saka takes French without any of English, Physics or Mathematics.","passage":"A new secondary school just opened in your area and started off with 5 teachers teaching 6 subjects (Physics, English, Geography, Mathematics, History, French). The combinations of teachers and subjects are:\n• Ben and Tatiana teach Physics and English\n• Cody and Tatiana teach English and Geography\n• Daniel and Ben teach Mathematics and Physics\n• Saka and Tatiana teach History and French","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-57"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"How many teachers take both English and Mathematics?","opts":["4","2","1","3"],"ans":2,"exp":"Subjects per teacher: Tatiana — Physics, English, Geography, History, French (5); Ben — Physics, English, Mathematics (3); Cody — English, Geography (2); Daniel — Mathematics, Physics (2); Saka — History, French (2). Only Ben teaches both English and Mathematics.","passage":"A new secondary school just opened in your area and started off with 5 teachers teaching 6 subjects (Physics, English, Geography, Mathematics, History, French). The combinations of teachers and subjects are:\n• Ben and Tatiana teach Physics and English\n• Cody and Tatiana teach English and Geography\n• Daniel and Ben teach Mathematics and Physics\n• Saka and Tatiana teach History and French","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-58"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Who is taller than Craig but shorter than Bob?","opts":["Trump","Mohammed","Clinton","Not enough information","None of the options"],"ans":2,"exp":"Craig is shorter than Clinton, and Bob is the tallest, so Clinton is taller than Craig and shorter than Bob. Trump and Mohammed are not above Craig.","passage":"There are five friends (Trump, Clinton, Bob, Craig and Mohammed) who kept arguing about who was the tallest or the shortest due to the closeness of their heights.\n• Trump is shorter than Clinton but a bit taller than Mohammed\n• Bob is the tallest\n• Craig is a bit shorter than Clinton but taller than Mohammed","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-59"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Who is the shortest of the friends?","opts":["Clinton","Craig","Trump","None of the options","Mohammed"],"ans":4,"exp":"Trump and Craig are both taller than Mohammed; Clinton is taller than both of them; Bob is tallest. Mohammed is shortest.","passage":"There are five friends (Trump, Clinton, Bob, Craig and Mohammed) who kept arguing about who was the tallest or the shortest due to the closeness of their heights.\n• Trump is shorter than Clinton but a bit taller than Mohammed\n• Bob is the tallest\n• Craig is a bit shorter than Clinton but taller than Mohammed","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-60"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Which two students do not wear glasses and aren’t tall?","opts":["Frank and Elias","Sean and Bamford","Bamford and Elias","Bamford and Frank"],"ans":2,"exp":"No glasses: Bamford, Frank, Elias. Tall: Curtis and Frank. Removing Frank leaves Bamford and Elias.","passage":"Six students of the University of Lagos are at the sports centre for a sport event. Sean and Bamford are from Shodeinde hall, while the rest (Curtis, Frank, Elias and David) are from Jaja hall, which is usually reserved for first class students. Curtis and Frank are taller than everyone else in the group. Bamford, Frank and Elias do not wear glasses, unlike the rest.","note":"None of the original options (Frank and Elias / Sean and Bamford / David and Frank / Bamford and Frank) was correct. Option C now carries the correct pair.","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-61"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Amanda will audition on what day?","opts":["Monday","Sunday","Saturday","Friday"],"ans":0,"exp":"Amanda auditions on the first day of the week: Monday.","passage":"A film company is holding auditions on all seven days of one week (Monday to Sunday). Valentine, Augustine, Freddy, Mandy, Sandy, Amanda and Curry are lined up, one per day.\n• Mandy is to audition during the middle of the week\n• Valentine is to audition on a weekend\n• Sandy is to audition just before Mandy\n• Amanda is to audition on the first day of the week\n• Freddy is to audition after Mandy\n• Augustine should come between Freddy and Valentine","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-62"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"If it takes 12 men a total of 8 hours to dig a hole and fill it to the brim with water, how long will it take 24 men to dig half a hole and fill it with granite?","opts":["3 hours, 2 minutes","4 hours","3 hours","Incomplete information"],"ans":3,"exp":"A trick question: there is no such thing as “half a hole” (any hole is a whole hole), and nothing tells you how long filling with granite takes compared with water. The data cannot give a time.","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-63"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"How many people must alight before Barry can alight?","opts":["2","4","3","1"],"ans":3,"exp":"Kennedy alights before everyone. No other rule puts anyone ahead of Barry (Scholes must come after him, Isaac after Scholes). So only 1 person must alight first.","passage":"Six friends — Kennedy, Barry, Scholes, Isaac, Sherry and Garfield — boarded a bus home, and each alights at a different bus stop:\n• Garfield is to alight before Sherry\n• Kennedy alights before the others\n• Isaac alights after Scholes\n• Scholes cannot alight before Barry","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-64"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Who gets to alight before Kennedy?","opts":["Scholes","No one","Barry","Sherry"],"ans":1,"exp":"Kennedy alights before all the others, so no one alights before Kennedy.","passage":"Six friends — Kennedy, Barry, Scholes, Isaac, Sherry and Garfield — boarded a bus home, and each alights at a different bus stop:\n• Garfield is to alight before Sherry\n• Kennedy alights before the others\n• Isaac alights after Scholes\n• Scholes cannot alight before Barry","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-65"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Michelle is likely the child of who?","opts":["Oscar","Solomon","Simbi","Terry"],"ans":0,"exp":"Oscar and Brian are the matriarch and patriarch, and Michelle is the sister of Solomon and Simbi — the children. So Michelle is Oscar and Brian’s child.","passage":"In a family of six members (Solomon, Simbi, Oscar, Brian, Terry and Michelle), there are two married couples, although one member is not biological. Oscar and Brian are married and are the matriarch and patriarch of the family. Solomon is related to Simbi, while Michelle is the female child and a sister to Solomon and Simbi.","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-66"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Who is likely the non-biological member of the family?","opts":["Terry","Oscar","None of the options","Solomon"],"ans":0,"exp":"Oscar and Brian are the parents; Solomon, Simbi and Michelle are their children. The second married couple must include Terry married to Solomon or Simbi, so Terry is the in-law — the non-biological member.","passage":"In a family of six members (Solomon, Simbi, Oscar, Brian, Terry and Michelle), there are two married couples, although one member is not biological. Oscar and Brian are married and are the matriarch and patriarch of the family. Solomon is related to Simbi, while Michelle is the female child and a sister to Solomon and Simbi.","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-67"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"I am introduced to the cousin of my brother’s cousin, who is a relative of my father. Who is this person to me?","opts":["Nephew","Sister","Cousin","Niece"],"ans":2,"exp":"My brother’s cousin is also my cousin. That cousin’s cousin on my father’s side is a child of my father’s sibling — my cousin too.","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-68"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"What is the relation between Fego and Esse?","opts":["Uncle","Daughter","Husband","Son"],"ans":3,"exp":"Deedee is the father of Abbey and grandfather of Fego, so Abbey is Fego’s parent — the two fathers are Deedee and Abbey. Esse is the mother, married to Abbey (the “husband of Esse” that Brad is related to). The three brothers are Abbey and Esse’s sons: Brad, Fego and Cooper. Fego is Esse’s son.","passage":"There are six individuals: Abbey, Brad, Cooper, Deedee, Esse and Fego. Brad is related to the husband of Esse. Deedee is the father of Abbey and the grandfather of Fego. In all there are two fathers, a mother and three brothers.","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-69"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Which of the following are brothers?","opts":["Abbey, Brad and Deedee","Abbey, Brad and Fego","Brad, Fego and Cooper","Brad, Deedee and Fego"],"ans":2,"exp":"Deedee is the father of Abbey and grandfather of Fego, so Abbey is Fego’s parent — the two fathers are Deedee and Abbey. Esse is the mother, married to Abbey (the “husband of Esse” that Brad is related to). The three brothers are Abbey and Esse’s sons: Brad, Fego and Cooper.","passage":"There are six individuals: Abbey, Brad, Cooper, Deedee, Esse and Fego. Brad is related to the husband of Esse. Deedee is the father of Abbey and the grandfather of Fego. In all there are two fathers, a mother and three brothers.","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-70"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"How many children belong to Amanda?","opts":["One","Four","Three","Two"],"ans":3,"exp":"Mandy is Amanda’s daughter. Curry is the child of Sandy, Amanda’s husband (Sandy is not the mom, so Amanda is). That makes two children.","passage":"A family consists of six members of the same lineage: Amanda, Curry, Sandy, Mandy, Freddy and Augustine. Curry is a child of Sandy, however Sandy is not the mom of Curry. Amanda and Sandy are married to each other, while Freddy is the brother of Sandy. Mandy is the daughter of Amanda, while Augustine is brother to Amanda.","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-71"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Curry is the child of which father?","opts":["Augustine","None of the options","Sandy","Amanda"],"ans":2,"exp":"Curry is a child of Sandy, and Sandy is not his mom — so Sandy is his father.","passage":"A family consists of six members of the same lineage: Amanda, Curry, Sandy, Mandy, Freddy and Augustine. Curry is a child of Sandy, however Sandy is not the mom of Curry. Amanda and Sandy are married to each other, while Freddy is the brother of Sandy. Mandy is the daughter of Amanda, while Augustine is brother to Amanda.","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-72"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Which of the following is a brother-in-law to Sandy?","opts":["Amanda","Mandy","Augustine","Freddy"],"ans":2,"exp":"Augustine is the brother of Sandy’s wife Amanda, so he is Sandy’s brother-in-law. Freddy is Sandy’s own brother.","passage":"A family consists of six members of the same lineage: Amanda, Curry, Sandy, Mandy, Freddy and Augustine. Curry is a child of Sandy, however Sandy is not the mom of Curry. Amanda and Sandy are married to each other, while Freddy is the brother of Sandy. Mandy is the daughter of Amanda, while Augustine is brother to Amanda.","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-73"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"How is 584365 coded?","opts":["QQPRPR","RYQAPR","RQYAPR","ROYAPR"],"ans":2,"exp":"5 = R, 8 = Q, 4 = Y, 3 = A, 6 = P, 5 = R → RQYAPR.","passage":"Use this code:\n| 3 | 6 | 8 | 5 | 9 | 2 | 7 | 4 |\n| A | P | Q | R | T | Z | W | Y |","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-74"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"How is PQZWYAT written in numbers?","opts":["9347286","6827439","4394682","4793286"],"ans":1,"exp":"P = 6, Q = 8, Z = 2, W = 7, Y = 4, A = 3, T = 9 → 6827439.","passage":"Use this code:\n| 3 | 6 | 8 | 5 | 9 | 2 | 7 | 4 |\n| A | P | Q | R | T | Z | W | Y |","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-75"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"How is 665243 coded?","opts":["PPYZYA","PPRZZA","PPRZYA","PPYRZA"],"ans":2,"exp":"6 = P, 6 = P, 5 = R, 2 = Z, 4 = Y, 3 = A → PPRZYA.","passage":"Use this code:\n| 3 | 6 | 8 | 5 | 9 | 2 | 7 | 4 |\n| A | P | Q | R | T | Z | W | Y |","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-76"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"If 74882 is coded as QURENO and 6820 is coded as RENT, how is 24782 coded?","opts":["NUEQN","NQUEN","QNEUN","NUQEN"],"ans":3,"exp":"From RENT: 8 = E, 2 = N, 0 = T. From QURENO: 7 = Q, 4 = U. So 2-4-7-8-2 = N-U-Q-E-N.","note":"The source code QURENO has one letter more than the five digits it encodes, so only the letters that agree across both examples are used.","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-77"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Look carefully at the pattern and determine which numbers come next:\n18 16 19 5 17 20 5 18 …","opts":["16 5","16 4","15 4","21 5"],"ans":3,"exp":"Read it in groups of three: (18, 16, 19), (5, 17, 20), (5, 18, ?). The middle terms go 16, 17, 18 and the last terms go 19, 20, 21, while each later group starts with 5. So the next two terms are 21, then 5 (start of the next group).","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-78"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Looking at this series: 45, 43, 47, 45, 49, … Which number should come next?","opts":["47","44","46","51"],"ans":0,"exp":"The steps alternate −2, +4: 45 → 43 → 47 → 45 → 49 → 47.","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-79"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Select the right number after studying the series: 15, 5, 20, 15, 35, …","opts":["20","25","15","5"],"ans":1,"exp":"Every third term is the sum of the two before it: 15 + 5 = 20, 20 + 15 = 35. The “added” terms rise by 10: 5, 15, 25. So the next term is 25 (and then 35 + 25 = 60).","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-80"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Which student excels at the most number of subjects?","opts":["None of the options","Gbogo","Victor","Emiola"],"ans":0,"exp":"Farouk: Civic, Health, Commerce. Victor: Civic, Health, Commerce. Emiola: Health, Commerce, Psychology. Ayodeji: Health, Commerce, Civic. Gbogo: Health, Commerce, Psychology. All five students excel at exactly 3 subjects, so no single student has the most.","passage":"The following is the distribution of subjects in which 5 students excel in secondary school.\n• Farouk and Victor excel at Civic Education and Health Education\n• Emiola and Farouk do really well at Health Education and Commerce\n• Emiola, Ayodeji and Gbogo are good in Health Education and Commerce\n• Gbogo and Emiola are good at Health Education and Psychology\n• Ayodeji and Victor always get high grades at Commerce and Civic Education","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-81"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"How many students excel at Psychology?","opts":["1","None of the options","3","2"],"ans":3,"exp":"Farouk: Civic, Health, Commerce. Victor: Civic, Health, Commerce. Emiola: Health, Commerce, Psychology. Ayodeji: Health, Commerce, Civic. Gbogo: Health, Commerce, Psychology. Psychology: Gbogo and Emiola — 2 students.","passage":"The following is the distribution of subjects in which 5 students excel in secondary school.\n• Farouk and Victor excel at Civic Education and Health Education\n• Emiola and Farouk do really well at Health Education and Commerce\n• Emiola, Ayodeji and Gbogo are good in Health Education and Commerce\n• Gbogo and Emiola are good at Health Education and Psychology\n• Ayodeji and Victor always get high grades at Commerce and Civic Education","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-82"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"A man points out a portrait saying, “This is the daughter of the only son of my father’s wife.” Who is this person to the man?","opts":["Sister","Daughter","Cousin","Mother"],"ans":1,"exp":"My father’s wife is my mother; her only son is me. The daughter of that only son is my daughter.","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-83"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"At a party, Sharon points out a dancing figure saying, “He is the only surviving son of the father of my sister.” Who is this person to Sharon?","opts":["Sister","None of the above","Uncle","Mother"],"ans":1,"exp":"The father of Sharon’s sister is Sharon’s father; his only surviving son is Sharon’s brother. “Brother” is not offered, so “None of the above”.","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-84"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Which two LCDAs can be said to be popular?","opts":["B3/B6","B1/B4","B1/B6","B1/B3","B2/B6"],"ans":0,"exp":"B1 and B4 are stated to be not popular, and B2 and B5 are not popular landmarks. The only LCDAs never described as unpopular are B3 and B6.","passage":"There are six LCDAs: B1, B2, B3, B4, B5, B6.\n• B1 is not an urban bus stop\n• B2 and B5 are not popular landmarks\n• B4 is not a commercial place\n• B1 and B4 are not popular places\n• B1 and B2 are very dissimilar","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-85"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Which entrants play only two positions?","opts":["Ferdinand","Inzaghi","Pirlo and Ferdinand","Pirlo"],"ans":2,"exp":"Pirlo: striker, midfield (2). Ferdinand: defender, left wing (2). Inzaghi: striker, midfield, defender (3). Zokora: left wing, midfield, striker (3). Dembele: left wing, midfield, striker, defender (4). Pirlo and Ferdinand each play exactly two.","passage":"CR7 football academy has accepted five new entrants: Pirlo, Ferdinand, Inzaghi, Zokora and Dembele. Their positions:\n• Pirlo and Inzaghi play striker and midfield\n• Ferdinand plays defender and left wing\n• Zokora and Dembele play left wing, midfield and striker\n• Inzaghi and Dembele play defender\n• Ferdinand and Zokora play left wing","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-86"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Which entrants play midfield?","opts":["None of the options","Inzaghi and Zokora","Pirlo, Inzaghi and Zokora"],"ans":0,"exp":"Pirlo: striker, midfield (2). Ferdinand: defender, left wing (2). Inzaghi: striker, midfield, defender (3). Zokora: left wing, midfield, striker (3). Dembele: left wing, midfield, striker, defender (4). Midfield: Pirlo, Inzaghi, Zokora and Dembele. No option lists all four, so “None of the options”.","passage":"CR7 football academy has accepted five new entrants: Pirlo, Ferdinand, Inzaghi, Zokora and Dembele. Their positions:\n• Pirlo and Inzaghi play striker and midfield\n• Ferdinand plays defender and left wing\n• Zokora and Dembele play left wing, midfield and striker\n• Inzaghi and Dembele play defender\n• Ferdinand and Zokora play left wing","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-87"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"If all the statements are true, which of the following must also be true?","opts":["Fewer self-centred parents than generous parents are good listeners","Some parents are self-centred and good listeners","All parents who are good listeners are good parents","Most parents who are not good listeners are not generous"],"ans":1,"exp":"Some self-centred parents are good parents, and all good parents are good listeners — so those self-centred parents are good listeners (B). C reverses the conditional; A and D cannot be derived.","passage":"Most parents who are generous to their wards are good parents, but some who are self-centred are also good parents. However, if there is one characteristic that all good parents share, it is that they are all good listeners.","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-88"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"Which of the following is an assumption required by the argument?","opts":["The relative smallness of certain parts of the brain is not a result of schizophrenia","When identical twins suffer from schizophrenia, the brains of both of them are the same size","The brain structure of a schizophrenic is smaller than the brain of someone not suffering from schizophrenia","Identical twins will suffer from the same symptoms as paternal twins"],"ans":0,"exp":"The argument treats the smaller brain areas as the cause of schizophrenia. That only works if the shrinkage is not an effect of the illness — the assumption in A. Without it, the causation could run the other way.","passage":"The brains of identical twins are amazingly identical. Studies have shown that when one of a pair of identical twins is schizophrenic, some areas of the affected twin’s brain are smaller than the corresponding areas in the brain of the unaffected twin. These differences are not found in non-schizophrenic twins. This evidence points to the fact that schizophrenia is caused by extensive damage to the physical structure of the brain.","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-89"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"If the statements on which the conclusion is based are all true, each of the following could be true EXCEPT:","opts":["Brain abnormalities associated with schizophrenia are caused by childhood infections that hinder the development of brain cells","Sooner or later it will become possible to identify whether a person will develop schizophrenia based on genetic information alone","Schizophrenia is a disease that is highly prevalent among twins","People who lack immunity to schizophrenia will eventually develop the disease over time"],"ans":1,"exp":"Identical twins share the same genes, yet only one of them develops schizophrenia. So genetic information alone cannot predict who develops it — B cannot be true. A, C and D are compatible with the facts.","passage":"The brains of identical twins are amazingly identical. Studies have shown that when one of a pair of identical twins is schizophrenic, some areas of the affected twin’s brain are smaller than the corresponding areas in the brain of the unaffected twin. These differences are not found in non-schizophrenic twins. This evidence points to the fact that schizophrenia is caused by extensive damage to the physical structure of the brain.","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-90"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Which of the following must be true?","opts":["E is assigned to an even-numbered stall","K is assigned to stall 1","I is assigned to stall 2 or 4","G is assigned to an odd-numbered stall"],"ans":2,"exp":"I cannot be in 1 (a tiger is there), 3 (faces G in 6) or 6 (G). If I were in 5, H would need stall 6, which is G’s. So I is in 2 (H in 3) or 4 (H in 5). B and D are impossible; A is not forced.","passage":"Four tigers — E, F, G, H — and two leopards — I and K — will be sent to exactly six stalls, one animal per stall.\nFirst row: 1 2 3\nSecond row: 4 5 6\nStalls facing each other: 1 and 4, 2 and 5, 3 and 6. Conditions:\n• The leopards’ stalls cannot face each other\n• A tiger must be assigned to stall 1\n• G must be assigned to stall 6\n• H must be assigned to the stall numbered one higher than I’s stall\n• I’s stall cannot face G’s stall","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-91"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"The earliest track that can be filled by G is the:","opts":["Fifth","Third","Sixth","Fourth"],"ans":3,"exp":"G needs C and F before it, and track 2 is A. The earliest is C on 1, A on 2, F on 3, G on 4. The rest fits: e.g. B 5, D 6, E 7 (D not 7th, B not next to C). So the fourth track.","passage":"Music producer Yanny is placing seven pieces — A, B, C, D, E, F, G — one per track on a seven-track recording, under these conditions:\n• A must be second\n• D cannot be seventh\n• B can come neither immediately before nor immediately after C\n• C must be on some track before F\n• F must be on some track before G","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-92"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Which one of the following could be the order, from first to seventh, of the pieces on the recording?","opts":["A, B, H, D, E, G, C","B, A, C, E, F, D, G","G, E, F, A, C, B, D","A, B, C, D, E, G, F"],"ans":1,"exp":"A: A is not second and there is no piece H. C: A is not second and G is before C and F. D: A is not second and G comes before F. B satisfies every rule: A second, D sixth, B (1st) not next to C (3rd), C before F before G.","passage":"Music producer Yanny is placing seven pieces — A, B, C, D, E, F, G — one per track on a seven-track recording, under these conditions:\n• A must be second\n• D cannot be seventh\n• B can come neither immediately before nor immediately after C\n• C must be on some track before F\n• F must be on some track before G","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-93"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Which of the following must be true?","opts":["Westlife performs earlier than One Direction","Plantation Boys perform in either slot five or six","Only Style-Plus can perform in a later slot than Plantation Boys","Plantation Boys perform either immediately before or immediately after Style-Plus"],"ans":1,"exp":"Backstreet Boys, One Direction and Westlife must all precede Plantation Boys. If Plantation Boys were 4th, those three plus Bracket would need the first three slots — impossible. So Plantation Boys are 5th or 6th.","passage":"A promoter is filling six slots (1 = earliest, 6 = latest) at an NGO concert with six bands: Style-Plus, Backstreet Boys, Westlife, Plantation Boys, Bracket and One Direction.\n• Backstreet Boys perform earlier than One Direction\n• Westlife and One Direction each perform earlier than Plantation Boys\n• Style-Plus performs in one of the last three slots\n• Bracket performs in one of the first three slots","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-94"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"How many individuals are faster than Kehinde?","opts":["4","1","2","3"],"ans":0,"exp":"Speed order (fastest first): Solomon > Dada > Akinbode > Akinfala > Kehinde. The survival facts are distractors. Everyone else (4 people) is faster than Kehinde.","passage":"Five brothers wish to undertake a trip to the Kalahari desert. The following is a measure of how well each is able to run in the desert for 7 days straight.\n• Akinbode is faster than Akinfala\n• Dada is faster than Akinbode but slower than Solomon\n• Solomon is faster than Kehinde, who is slower than Akinfala\n• Akinfala can last three days without water\n• Akinbode can last 4 days without food and water\n• Someone dies before the end of the 7th day","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-95"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"If the fastest person in the group is removed, who becomes the fastest?","opts":["Kehinde","Dada","Akinfala","Solomon"],"ans":1,"exp":"Speed order (fastest first): Solomon > Dada > Akinbode > Akinfala > Kehinde. The survival facts are distractors. Remove Solomon and Dada is fastest.","passage":"Five brothers wish to undertake a trip to the Kalahari desert. The following is a measure of how well each is able to run in the desert for 7 days straight.\n• Akinbode is faster than Akinfala\n• Dada is faster than Akinbode but slower than Solomon\n• Solomon is faster than Kehinde, who is slower than Akinfala\n• Akinfala can last three days without water\n• Akinbode can last 4 days without food and water\n• Someone dies before the end of the 7th day","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-96"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"Amaechi can be said to be committed to which of the following?","opts":["Amaechi is the best young candidate equipped with understanding the issues facing the country","Addressing the problems of the country needs the understanding of youths","A younger person, younger than Daura, is needed to effectively address the difficult issues facing the country","Many old people in general cannot address the problems facing the country"],"ans":2,"exp":"Amaechi says Daura is too old for the job and it should be left to younger people. That commits him to C. He never claims to be the best candidate (A), never generalises about all old people (D), and B is broader than what he said.","passage":"Amaechi: My distinguished opponent, you are much too old to address the pressing issues facing this country such as power, poverty and unemployment. Leave this to the younger ones like us.\nDaura: I will refrain from making age an issue during this election campaign, so I will not be tempted to make any remarks on your relative youth and inexperience in handling government matters.","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-97"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Which of the students takes all four courses?","opts":["Einstein","Freud","Bretano","Shakur"],"ans":0,"exp":"Einstein: 111, 112, 113, 114. Shakur: 112, 113, 114. Bretano: 111, 112, 113. Freud: 111, 113. Only Einstein takes all four.","passage":"Four friends (Einstein, Shakur, Bretano and Freud) are taking courses in advanced coding (111, 112, 113, 114):\n• Einstein, Bretano and Freud take 111\n• Shakur, Bretano and Freud take 113\n• Einstein and Shakur take 113 and 114\n• Einstein, Shakur and Bretano take 112","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-98"},{"cat":"Logical Reasoning","q":"Which of the students does not take course 111?","opts":["Bretano","Shakur","Freud","Einstein and Shakur"],"ans":1,"exp":"Einstein: 111, 112, 113, 114. Shakur: 112, 113, 114. Bretano: 111, 112, 113. Freud: 111, 113. Only Shakur is missing from 111.","passage":"Four friends (Einstein, Shakur, Bretano and Freud) are taking courses in advanced coding (111, 112, 113, 114):\n• Einstein, Bretano and Freud take 111\n• Shakur, Bretano and Freud take 113\n• Einstein and Shakur take 113 and 114\n• Einstein, Shakur and Bretano take 112","src":"EY_BANK+new","id":"ey-99"}];
